+++ b/docs/PROFILE_GUIDE.md
@@ -83,7 +83,11 @@ For a picture of the port's own beside one of the game's screens, such as a hint
 
 `glyph_cache` is for changing the font while a game runs. A game that caches the glyphs it has drawn keeps them until its atlas needs the cell, so a new font shows only in text it has not drawn yet. Where the game keeps that atlas, read off its own text code, lets the framework make it draw them again. Null for any game nobody has traced.
 
-Sharp text needs nothing from the profile. A game that draws the buffer it hands `sceFontGetCharGlyphImage` as a texture, as Dissidia 012 does with each block of text, gets that text drawn again at the internal resolution (`host/fonts/sharp_text.hpp`): the framework keeps each glyph it draws into a buffer, and when the renderer loads a 4- or 8-bit texture lying in one that holds exactly what the glyphs left, it draws the same glyphs that many times larger. A buffer the game also wrote itself, or one drawn with another font, keeps the game's texture, and a game that copies its glyphs on into an atlas never draws the buffer, so it is drawn as before. The Video page's Text section has **Sharp text** (`text.sharp`, on); `<prefix>_NO_SHARP_TEXT=1` turns it off for a comparison, `<prefix>_SHARP_TEXT_MEMORY=<MB>` sets the GPU memory such text may use (256 MB; past it, text not drawn in the last two frames goes first, and new text is drawn from the game's texture when none can), and `<prefix>_TRACE_SHARP_TEXT=1` logs each texture looked up and why one is not drawn sharp.
+Sharp text needs nothing from the profile. A game that draws the buffer it hands `sceFontGetCharGlyphImage` as a texture, as Dissidia 012 does with each block of text, gets that text drawn at the internal resolution (`host/fonts/sharp_text.hpp`): the framework keeps each glyph it draws into a buffer, and when the renderer meets a 4- or 8-bit texture lying in one that holds exactly what the glyphs left, it draws the same glyphs at the size they have on the screen. A buffer the game also wrote itself, or one drawn with another font, keeps the game's texture, and a game that copies its glyphs on into an atlas never draws the buffer, so it is drawn as before.
+
+Most such draws are **native text**: an upright quad showing part of the buffer, its texture modulated by the vertex colour with an alpha test of "greater" and a palette of one colour whose alpha rises with the index (a glyph's coverage), is drawn as its glyphs from a glyph atlas in the quad's place, with its state and colour, each glyph cut at the quad's edges. A new text then costs no texture at all: a game that makes 20 or more texts in one frame (a list scrolled by holding a direction) no longer stalls on making their textures. A quad that can draw nothing (no alpha, wholly outside the scissor, or no area) is skipped. The atlas has two pages (2048 pixels square up to 4x, 4096 above): when the current one is full, the other, once no frame drew from it for six frames, is cleared and takes over; until then new text is drawn from a texture. Any other draw of a glyph buffer is drawn from a texture, the glyphs drawn again that many times larger (Sharp text's pictures). A texture pack does not replace text drawn natively, and **Dump textures** does not write it.
+
+The Video page's Text section has **Sharp text** (`text.sharp`, on), which turns both on and off. `<prefix>_NO_SHARP_TEXT=1` turns it off for a comparison and `<prefix>_NO_NATIVE_TEXT=1` keeps Sharp text's pictures for every draw; `<prefix>_SHARP_TEXT_MEMORY=<MB>` sets the GPU memory those pictures may use (256 MB; past it, text not drawn in the last two frames goes first, and new text is drawn from the game's texture when none can); `<prefix>_TRACE_SHARP_TEXT=1` logs each texture looked up and why one is not drawn sharp, and `<prefix>_TRACE_NATIVE_TEXT=1` counts every 300 frames what native text drew, skipped and declined.
 
 `sharp_textures` lists pixel art to draw with nearest filtering whatever the Texture filter setting says, for a sprite that smoothing blurs at a higher resolution. Each is a `SharpTexture`: its key as **Dump textures** names the file (the address ignored, xxh64), and its width and height. Only a texture of a listed size is hashed, when it is uploaded, so the list costs nothing for the others. A key covers one texture with one palette, so a sprite sheet loaded with another palette, or as a different part of its picture, has a key of its own: dump each scene it appears in. `<prefix>_NO_SHARP_PIXEL_ART=1` draws them as the setting says, for a comparison. A texture pack's image for a listed texture keeps the pack's own filtering. Without any, the setting decides for every texture.
 
diff --git a/host/fonts/game_font.cpp b/host/fonts/game_font.cpp
index 6318dec..5fcdfc1 100644
--- a/host/fonts/game_font.cpp
+++ b/host/fonts/game_font.cpp
@@ -650,6 +650,49 @@ GlyphBitmap render_scaled(std::uint32_t code, std::int32_t x64, std::int32_t y64
     return placed;
 }
 
+bool place(std::uint32_t code, std::int32_t x64, std::int32_t y64, GlyphPlace &out) {
+    State &s = loaded_state();
+    const Layout &layout = layout_for(s, code);
+    if (layout.face == nullptr || layout.metrics.width == 0) return false;
+    // As in render_scaled(): the 1x bitmap's natural box goes at (x64, y64).
+    const float x = static_cast<float>(x64) / 64.0f;
+    const float y = static_cast<float>(y64) / 64.0f;
+    out.origin_x = x - static_cast<float>(layout.natural_x0);
+    out.origin_y = y - static_cast<float>(layout.natural_y0);
+    // A subpixel shift moves the bitmap by up to a pixel either way.
+    out.x0 = x - 1.0f;
+    out.y0 = y - 1.0f;
+    out.x1 = x + static_cast<float>(layout.metrics.width) + 1.0f;
+    out.y1 = y + static_cast<float>(layout.metrics.height) + 1.0f;
+    return true;
+}
+
+GlyphBitmap render_at(std::uint32_t code, float scale_x, float scale_y, float shift_x) {
+    State &s = loaded_state();
+    const Layout &layout = layout_for(s, code);
+    GlyphBitmap bitmap;
+    if (layout.face == nullptr || layout.metrics.width == 0 || !(scale_x > 0.0f) || !(scale_y > 0.0f) ||
+        scale_x > 32.0f || scale_y > 32.0f)
+        return bitmap;
+    const float glyph_x = layout.scale_x * scale_x;
+    const float glyph_y = layout.scale_y * scale_y;
+    const float fx = std::clamp(shift_x, 0.0f, 63.0f / 64.0f);
+    int x0 = 0, y0 = 0, x1 = 0, y1 = 0;
+    stbtt_GetGlyphBitmapBoxSubpixel(&layout.face->info, layout.glyph, glyph_x, glyph_y, fx, 0.0f, &x0, &y0, &x1, &y1);
+    bitmap.width = std::max(x1 - x0, 0);
+    bitmap.height = std::max(y1 - y0, 0);
+    bitmap.x = x0;
+    bitmap.y = y0;
+    bitmap.pixels.assign(static_cast<std::size_t>(bitmap.width) * static_cast<std::size_t>(bitmap.height), 0u);
+    if (!bitmap.pixels.empty())
+        stbtt_MakeGlyphBitmapSubpixel(&layout.face->info, bitmap.pixels.data(), bitmap.width, bitmap.height,
+                                      bitmap.width, glyph_x, glyph_y, fx, 0.0f, layout.glyph);
+    // The 1x glyph's emboldening, as many of these pixels wide.
+    const int bold = static_cast<int>(std::lround(static_cast<float>(layout.bold) * scale_x));
+    if (bold > 0 && !bitmap.pixels.empty()) embolden(bitmap, bold);
+    return bitmap;
+}
+
 static void (*reload_hook)() = nullptr;
 
 void reload() {
